WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.860
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:12.660
Today is a good day. Scientists at NVIDIA have&nbsp;
done it. Now, I hear you asking, Doctor, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:18.960
are you talking about? Well, we like to say that&nbsp;
ray tracing is the holy grail of computer graphics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:26.460
research, and within ray tracing, wave-optical&nbsp;
light simulations perhaps are the holy grail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:33.000
Yes, the holy grail within the holy grail.&nbsp;
However, achieving that is really difficult.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:40.980
You see, ray tracing means writing a simulation&nbsp;
program that takes rays of light and bounces them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:47.100
around in the scene, creating a beautiful,&nbsp;
photorealistic image. Note that I used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:55.800
the word rays of light. However, light is an&nbsp;
electromagnetic wave. Yes a wave, not a ray.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:02.520
If we simulated it as a wave instead, it&nbsp;
could create a convincing image of incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:08.820
beautiful microscopic structures, like light&nbsp;
diffraction off of the scales of a Brazilian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:16.680
Rainbow Boa or a piece of Bornite ore. Or it&nbsp;
could create an image of beetle shells that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:22.440
have multiple layers that bounce light around&nbsp;
between them. These beautiful wave effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:29.940
can also be observed in the spoke guards of some&nbsp;
bicycles, and other parts as well. All beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:36.900
shiny rainbowy things that are unfortunately&nbsp;
almost impossible to simulate. This previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:43.860
technique from just a year ago can at least start&nbsp;
rendering the diffractive surface of a CD disk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:51.120
but look. This one should be a line, not&nbsp;
a collection of dots. So, unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:59.340
no wave-optics simulations for us. Until today.&nbsp;
Yes, scientists at NVIDIA introduced a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.800
technique that can simulate light&nbsp;
both as a ray, but as a wave too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:13.020
and this gives rise to these beautiful effects. Oh&nbsp;
my goodness, look at that gorgeous scene. So good!

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:21.360
And..yes! Finally, when it comes to CDs, rainbowy&nbsp;
lines are now lines, we can have our beetles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.780
our bornite ore, and even the snake. Now&nbsp;
hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:35.280
because here comes the best part, it can simulate&nbsp;
all of these phenomena up to 10,000 times faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.940
than previous techniques. And note that this&nbsp;
is a handcrafted technique. A product of pure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:50.160
human ingenuity. Wow. As we are approaching&nbsp;
superintelligence, I mean these AI things are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:57.600
starting to be able to decode Hungarian words,&nbsp;
yes, this is a Hungarian word, really, and yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:05.640
the translation is also correct. But such a pure&nbsp;
handcrafted technique is…chef’s kiss. Love it.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:12.000
And here is something that also made&nbsp;
me extremely happy. Look at this. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:19.020
is a new work on how to simulate the appearance of&nbsp;
glints, shiny little dotty things in a simulator.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:26.100
This can take car paint materials, snow,&nbsp;
shiny presents and create a next level video.&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:35.940
Up to 5x faster than the state of the art. And&nbsp;
does that mean that? Oh yes, yes it does! All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:43.920
this is happening in real time. Pinch me, I can’t&nbsp;
believe it. All this beautiful footage in real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:50.400
time. You see, there is a ton of action happening&nbsp;
in light transport simulation research, and these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:56.100
incredible papers just keep coming out one&nbsp;
after another. Breakthrough after breakthrough.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:02.520
Now, if you allow me, I would also like to show&nbsp;
you a really cool new paper from my colleague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:08.940
Christian Freude, who was not happy with the&nbsp;
way we compare the results of different ray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:15.960
tracing programs. You see, these two images were&nbsp;
computed differently, however, this renderer was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:22.740
cutting some corners. But where? If we use&nbsp;
the usual previous ways of comparing images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:31.620
we get some information, but not that much,&nbsp;
however, look. Haha! This new technique tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:36.720
us exactly where those corners were cut.
I was a small part of this paper too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.480
and if you wish to read it, make sure to check&nbsp;
it out in the video description. Bravo Christian!

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:45.420
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

